Take A01 Broken Access Control. The defensive reading is a checklist, not an exploit: (1) Every server route that returns or changes data checks 'is this user allowed to touch THIS object?' (2) Access is denied by default and only opened deliberately. (3) IDs in URLs are authorized server-side, never assumed safe. A tiny guard makes the intent explicit:
```js
// Express-style route — authorize before acting
app.get('/invoices/:id', requireLogin, async (req, res) => {
  const invoice = await db.getInvoice(req.params.id);
  if (!invoice || invoice.ownerId !== req.user.id) {
    return res.sendStatus(404); // deny by default; don't confirm existence
  }
  res.json(invoice);
});
```

Injection happens when untrusted data is mixed into a command's *syntax* — SQL, HTML, a shell — so the data can change what the command means. The cure is always the same: keep data and code in separate channels.

Vulnerable SQL (input becomes part of the query):
```js
// DO NOT DO THIS — string-built query
const row = await db.query(
  "SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '" + email + "'"
);
```
Secure SQL (parameterized — the driver sends data separately, so it can never be parsed as SQL):
```js
const row = await db.query(
  'SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1',
  [email]
);
```
Vulnerable HTML output (input rendered as active markup — XSS):
```js
// DO NOT DO THIS — innerHTML executes injected tags/scripts
el.innerHTML = 'Hi ' + username;
```
Secure output (treat input as text, and encode for the exact context):
```js
el.textContent = 'Hi ' + username; // browser renders it as plain text
```

Harden a search endpoint end to end. Use parameters for the query, validate the shape of input, and set a Content-Security-Policy so injected scripts cannot run even if something slips through:
```js
app.get('/search', async (req, res) => {
  const term = String(req.query.q ?? '').slice(0, 100); // bound the input
  const rows = await db.query(
    'SELECT id, title FROM docs WHERE title ILIKE $1 LIMIT 20',
    ['%' + term + '%']            // data stays a parameter
  );
  res.set('Content-Security-Policy', "default-src 'self'"); // defense in depth
  res.json(rows);                // JSON, not hand-built HTML
});
```
If you must build HTML on the server, render through a template engine that auto-escapes by default (e.g. output `{{ username }}` rather than raw interpolation), and reserve any 'raw HTML' helper for content you generated yourself.

Authentication proves *who* a user is; authorization decides *what* that user may do. Broken Access Control means the second check is missing, weak, or client-side. The most common form is an insecure direct object reference (IDOR): the server hands back an object because the ID exists, without checking who owns it.

Vulnerable (any logged-in user can read any record):
```js
app.get('/api/notes/:id', requireLogin, async (req, res) => {
  const note = await db.getNote(req.params.id); // no ownership check!
  res.json(note);
});
```
Secure (deny by default; verify ownership or role on this exact object):
```js
app.get('/api/notes/:id', requireLogin, async (req, res) => {
  const note = await db.getNote(req.params.id);
  if (!note || note.userId !== req.user.id) {
    return res.sendStatus(404); // don't reveal that it exists
  }
  res.json(note);
});
```

Centralize authorization so no route can forget it. Define what a role may do, then guard actions with a single helper:
```js
const can = {
  admin:  new Set(['note:read', 'note:write', 'note:delete']),
  member: new Set(['note:read', 'note:write']),
};
function authorize(permission) {
  return (req, res, next) => {
    if (!can[req.user.role]?.has(permission)) return res.sendStatus(403);
    next();
  };
}
// Server-side only. Never gate a sensitive action with a hidden UI flag.
app.delete('/api/notes/:id', requireLogin, authorize('note:delete'), handler);
```
Also enforce ownership in the data layer where possible — e.g. `DELETE FROM notes WHERE id = $1 AND user_id = $2` so a mismatched owner simply affects zero rows.

Authentication Failures (OWASP A07:2025) cover weak credentials storage, guessable-password tolerance, and leaky sessions. Passwords must be stored as a *salted, slow, one-way* hash so a database breach doesn't reveal them.

Vulnerable (reversible or fast hashing — both are wrong):
```js
user.password = encrypt(plain);        // reversible = catastrophic if leaked
// or
user.password = md5(plain);            // fast hash = trivially cracked
```
Secure (a purpose-built password hash with a per-user salt, built in):
```js
import argon2 from 'argon2';
// On signup:
user.passwordHash = await argon2.hash(plain); // salt + cost handled for you
// On login:
const ok = await argon2.verify(user.passwordHash, plain);
```
Use Argon2, scrypt, or bcrypt — never MD5/SHA-1, and never plain or reversible encryption.

Harden the session cookie and the login flow. Cookies that carry a session should be HttpOnly (JS can't read them, blunting XSS theft), Secure (HTTPS only), and SameSite (blunts CSRF):
```js
res.cookie('sid', sessionId, {
  httpOnly: true,
  secure: true,
  sameSite: 'lax',
  maxAge: 1000 * 60 * 60,   // short-lived; rotate on privilege change
});
```
At login, add rate limiting and a generic error so you don't reveal which field was wrong, and offer multi-factor authentication (MFA):
```js
if (!user || !(await argon2.verify(user.passwordHash, plain))) {
  await recordFailedAttempt(ip);        // throttle / back off repeated tries
  return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid email or password' });
}
regenerateSession(req);                 // new session id after login (anti-fixation)
```

Classify data, then protect accordingly. Don't store what you don't need (you can't leak a card number you never kept), and hash irreversible identifiers rather than encrypting them when you only need to compare:
```js
// Minimize: store a token from the payment processor, not the raw PAN.
user.cardToken = await processor.tokenize(cardInput); // no card data at rest

// Encrypt at rest for data you must recover (authenticated encryption):
import { randomBytes, createCipheriv } from 'node:crypto';
const key = Buffer.from(process.env.DATA_KEY, 'hex'); // from a secret manager
const iv = randomBytes(12);
const c = createCipheriv('aes-256-gcm', key, iv);
const enc = Buffer.concat([c.update(ssn, 'utf8'), c.final()]);
const tag = c.getAuthTag(); // store iv + tag + ciphertext together
```
Add a `.gitignore` for `.env`, scan history for leaked keys, and rotate any secret that was ever committed.
